  ],
  "text": "Rwanda delivers blood to remote clinics by drone. The same core technology is reshaping modern war. A drone is a very ordinary thing. Motors, a battery, a controller and a position fix. And not one of those parts cares what it is carrying. The blood delivery works because a road takes four hours and a flight takes fifteen minutes. The same class of airframe maps a farm, inspects a bridge, and searches a hillside for someone lost. And the same one, with something else strapped underneath, changes what a small force can do. The cost is the shift. Something that used to need an air force now needs a van. Which is exactly why the rules around them keep failing to keep up. The technology is genuinely indifferent. It holds no opinion about the payload. Which is what makes the rules the whole story, rather than the machine. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
